  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA troughing pattern begins to develop over the West Coast by \nFriday, which will help weaken the high pressure system further \nand bring in more cooling. High temperatures were raised slightly \nfrom the previous forecast, but still shows a steeper decline in \ntemperatures mainly west of the mountains from 5-10 degrees cooler\nthan today. A stronger low pressure system develops from this \ntroughing pattern to the north, swinging through the Pacific \nNorthwest this weekend. This will bring further cooling to our \nregion with temperatures lowering closer to normal for the middle \nof September. Welcome highs in the 80s/90s for western valleys, \nmost mountain areas, and locales in the high desert can be \nexpected. Overnight temperatures and humid conditions will also \ndecrease into the weekend as well as drier southwest flow moves \ninto our area. Morning temperatures in the 70s will be replaced \nwith 60s west of the mountains making it feel much more \ncomfortable for us. The marine layer will also slowly return for \ncoastal and western valley areas over the next few days as well.\n\nThe area of low pressure moves out of the Pacific Northwest by\nMonday, leaving behind a weak troughing pattern over the region\nfor at least the first half of next week. This will bring similar\ntemperatures each day with highs and lows near to slightly below \naverage with dry weather.",
